1) What is the difference between mkdir and touch?
ans: mkdir command is used to create a directory
     touch command is used to create an empty file
2) What is the difference between cp and mv?
ans: cp command is used to copy the content from one file to another file and both will exist in the system
     mv command is used to move entire file to another file and newly create file will exist in the system

3) What happens when you use cat > file.txt and the file already exists?
ans: when we use cat > file.txt, it will replace the existing content from the file with the new content that has been added

4)What happens when you use cat >> file.txt and the file already exists?
ans: it will append the new content into existing file

5)What does Ctrl+D do when using cat > file.txt?
ans: it will end the process and return the terminal

6)What is the difference between cat and tac?
ans: cat command is used to display the content of the file on the terminal
     tac command is used to display the content of file in reverse order, ex: last line will print first 
7)What is the purpose of head?
ans: head command is used to display the top lines of logs

8) What is the purpose of tail?
ans: tail command is used to display the last few lines of logs

9) How do you display only the first 5 lines of a file?
ans: I will use head -5 <file_name>

10) How do you display only the last 5 lines of a file?
ans: tail -5 <file_name>

11)What happens if you use mv to move a file to another directory?
ans : it will move the file from current dir to another dir and delete the file from existing dir

12) Can cp copy a directory? If yes, what option is generally required?
ans: yes, we can use cp -r <dir_name> < target_dir>

13) where do you find system log files? which directory?
ans: /var/log
